    "lawCode": "Rpld & add §215 sub 2, amd §216-b, Exec L; rpld & add §207-b sub 2 ¶a, St Fin L; amd §130, Civ Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5503\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the executive law, in relation to\nthe terms and conditions of employment for members of the collective\nnegotiating units consisting of troopers and commissioned and\nnon-commissioned officers in the division of state police; to amend\nthe executive law, in relation to salary schedules for such members of\nsuch units; to amend the state finance law, in relation to the\nemployee benefit fund for members of such units; to amend the civil\nservice law, in relation to salary schedules for members of the\ncollective negotiating unit designated as the professional, scientific\nand technical services unit; making an appropriation therefor; and to\nrepeal certain provisions of the executive law and the state finance\nlaw relating to the salary and benefits payable to members of the\ncollective bargaining units representing troopers, and commissioned\nand non-commissioned officers of the division of state police\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill would implement the provisions of a collective bargaining\nagreement between the Executive Branch of the State of New York and\nthe employee organization representing members of the collective\nnegotiating units consisting of Troopers and Commissioned and\nNon-Commissioned Officers in the Division of State Police (hereafter\n\"members of affected units\").\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would repeal Executive Law § 215(2) and replace\nit with new provisions that would increase the salary schedules for\nmembers of affected units. Basic annual salaries for members of\naffected units will increase by 3 percent, effective April 1, 2007; 3\npercent, effective April 1, 2008; 3 percent, effective April 1, 2009;\nand 4 percent effective April 1, 2010.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would repeal State Finance Law § 207-b(2)(a) and\nreplace it with a new provision that provides for continued payments\nto the Employee Benefit Fund at $49 per member per year for the period\nApril 1, 2007 to March 31, 2011.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would amend Civil Service Law § 130(2)(c)(4) to\nmake corrections in the salary schedule applicable to members of the\nprofessional, scientific, and technical services unit.\n\nSections 4 and 5 of the bill would amend Executive Law §§ 216-b(2) and\n(3) to provide an increased longevity award to members of affected\nunits in each year of the collective bargaining agreement. Effective\nApril 1, 2007, the current longevity system would be replaced and\nmembers of affected units would be entitled to receive longevity\nawards according to the following schedule: (a) 6 to 10 years of\nservice - $430; (b) 11 to 15 years of service - $480; and (c) 16 to 25\nyears of service - $530.  Effective April 1, 2008, such amounts will\nincrease to $460, $510, and $560, respectively. Effective April 1,\n2009, such amounts will increase to $500, $550, and $600,\nrespectively. Effective April 1, 2010, such amounts will increase to\n$540, $590, and $640 respectively.\n\nSection 6 of the bill would provide for a retroactive lump sum payment\nfor members of the collective negotiating unit consisting of Troopers\nin the Division of State Police who were at the Trainee 1 step at any\npoint between April 1, 2007 and September 16, 2008. Such members would\nreceive a 3 percent increase to the basic annual salary received at\nthe Trainee 1 rate for the period between April 1, 2007 and the\ncompletion of their service at the Trainee 1 step.\n\n\nSection 7 of the bill would continue the location pay benefit of $200\nfor members of affected units whose official workstation is Monroe\nCounty, and it would increase the location pay benefit provided to\nmembers of affected units whose official work stations are in New York\nCity or in the counties of Rockland, Westchester, Nassau and Suffolk\nCounties by the same percentage increases as applied to basic annual\nsalaries under section 1 of this bill, as follows: Effective April 1,\n2007, such amount will increase to $1,392. Effective April 1, 2008,\nsuch amount will increase to $1,434. Effective April 1, 2009, such\namount will increase to $1,477. Effective April 1, 2010, such amount\nwill increase to $1,536.\n\nSection 8 of the bill would increase the supplemental location pay\nbenefit, as follows: Effective April 1, 2007 members of affected units\nwho work in (a) Orange, Putnam or Dutchess would receive $1,160, (b)\nRockland or Westchester County, or the City of New York would receive\n$ 1,739, and (c) in Nassau or Suffolk County would receive $2,029.\nEffective April 1, 2008. such amounts will increase to $1,195, $1,791,\nand $2,090, respectively. Effective April 1, 2009, such amounts will\nincrease to $1,230, $1,845, and $2,153, respectively. Effective April\n1, 2010, such amounts will increase to $1,280, $1,918, and $2,239,\nrespectively.\n\nSection 9 of the bill would increase the additional compensation\nreceived by members of affected units in recognition of such members'\nexpanded duties by $1,200 annually. Effective April 1, 2007, such\namount will increase to $3,852. Effective April 1, 2008, such amount\nwill increase to $5,502. Effective April 1, 2009, such amount will\nincrease to $6,252. Effective April 1, 2010, such amount will increase\nto $7,452.\n\nSection 10 of the bill would establish an annual payment of $1,200,\neffective April 1, 2010, for members of affected units in recognition\nof the hazardous duty performed by such members.\n\nSection 11 of the bill would increase the command pay benefit paid to\nmembers of the collective negotiating unit consisting of Commissioned\nand Non-Commissioned Officers in the Division of State Police by the\nsame percentage increases as applied to basic annual salaries under\nsection 1 of the bill. Effective April 1, 2007, such amount will\nincrease to $289. Effective April 1, 2008, such amount will increase\nto $298. Effective April 1, 2009, such amount will increase to $307.\nEffective April 1, 2010, such amount will increase to $319.\n\nSection 12 of the bill would continue the committee to study and\nassess physical fitness standards for members of affected units.\nEffective March 31, 2003, members of affected units would receive a\nfitness bonus if found to be eligible against established standards.\nThis section also permits the Division of State Police and the\nemployee organization representing members of affected units to\nmutually agree to spend monies appropriated for these bonuses for\nother purposes.\n\nSection 13 would continue the committee on health benefits for the\ncollective negotiating unit consisting of Troopers in the Division of\nState Police for the period April 1, 2007 to March 31, 2011, funded at\na level of $12,500 per year. This section would also continue the\ncommittee on health benefits for the collective negotiating unit\nconsisting of Commissioned and Non-Commissioned Officers in the\nDivision of State Police for the period April 1, 2007 to March 31,\n2011, funded at the level of $5,000 per year.\n\nSection 14 would provide professional development funding for members\nof affected units, which supports the tuition reimbursement program,\nthe master's program and the employee assistance program. During the\n\nperiod April 1, 2007 to March 31, 2011, $92,400 will be provided\nannually to each unit for this purpose.\n\nSection 15 of the bill would provide for payments to members of\naffected units who possess or obtain a recognized degree. Such members\nshall continue to receive a lump sum payment of $250 for an\nAssociate's Degree, $500 for a Bachelor's Degree, and $750 for a\ngraduate degree.\n\nSection 16 of the bill would continue the member in charge of\nsatellite station compensation. Members of the collective negotiating\nunit consisting of Troopers in the Division of State Police who are\ndesignated \"members in charge\" of a satellite station shall continue\nto receive $435 per year.\n\nSection 17 of the bill would provide for continued payment for members\nof affected units who are required to work short swings. Effective\nMarch 31, 2003, members of affected units who are required to work\nshort swings shall receive compensation of $30 for each short swing\nthey are required to work and actually work.\n\nSection 18 of the bill would continue lump sum payments for unused\nsick leave at retirement, as provided in section 9 of Chapter 9 of the\nLaws of 2001 and in section 9 of Chapter 10 of the Laws of 2001.\nUnused sick days will be cashed out according to the following\nformula: the number of accumulated and unused sick days, not to exceed\n300, will be reduced by 165 with the result being multiplied by the\nmember's daily rate of pay. The lump sum payable will be equal to\none-fifth of such amount.\n\nSection 19 of the bill would continue the overtime meal allowance for\nmembers of affected units.\n\nSection 20 of the bill would provide that salary increases and benefit\nmodifications shall not be implemented until the Director of Employee\nRelations informs the Director of the Budget and the Comptroller that\nthe agreement that provides for these increases and modifications has\nbeen ratified.\n\nSection 21 of the bill would provide that appropriations contained\ntherein shall be available to the State for the payment and\npublication of grievance arbitration settlements and awards.\n\nSection 22 of the bill would provide the date on which members of\naffected units would be entitled to the salary increases provided by\nthis bill.\n\nSection 23 of the bill would provide that until payment is made\npursuant to this bill, members of affected units shall receive such\npayment as is currently provided for their respective positions. When\nbasic annual salaries are first paid pursuant to this bill, such\nmembers shall be entitled to a lump sum payment for the difference\nbetween the salaries to which they were entitled since April 1, 2007\nand the compensation they actually received.\n\nSection 24 would authorize the Comptroller to pay any amounts required\nby the provisions of the bill. To the extent that existing\nappropriations available to any state department or agency are\ninsufficient to accomplish the purposes set forth in this section, the\nDirector of the Budget would be authorized to allocate to the various\ndepartments and agencies the amounts necessary to make such payments.\n\nSection 25 would appropriate monies to pay for personal services,\nother than personal services and fringe benefits, and to carry out the\nprovisions of this act.\n\nSection 26 would provide that the bill take effect immediately and be\ndeemed to have been in full force and effect on and after April 1,\n2007.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nChapter 157 of the Laws of 2005 addresses a variety of terms and\nconditions of employment established by the previous collective\nbargaining agreement for the members of these units for the period of\nApril 1, 2003 to March 31, 2007.\n\nExecutive Law §215 establishes the current salary schedules for\nmembers of this unit.\n\nState Finance Law §207-b authorizes payments to an employee benefit\nfund on behalf of members of affected units under interest arbitration\nawards and agreements.\n\nExecutive Law § 216-b(2) establishes the longevity award schedule for\nTroopers in the Division of State Police. Executive Law § 216-b(3)\nestablishes the longevity schedule for Commissioned and\nNon-Commissioned Officers in the Division of State Police.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNone.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nThis bill is necessary to implement the terms of collective bargaining\nagreements negotiated and agreed to by the Executive Branch of the\nState of New York and the employee organization representing the\naffected units.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThis bill includes $105.41 million in All Funds appropriations ($90.5\nmillion in General Fund) to support the costs of this act during the\n2009-10 fiscal year. The 2009-10 financial plan accommodates the costs\nassociated with this bill.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect immediately and would be deemed to have\nbeen in full force and effect on and after April 1, 2007.\n",
